**Describe the bug**
Initialize ultracite and use the eslint plugin setup as well as anti-slop. You will see in selectedJsPlugins that anti-slop complains about typeof plugin === "string".
...
> A `typeof` check narrows a representation without establishing its contract. Parse input at its I/O boundary, then branch on the domain value.oxc(anti-slop(no-runtime-typeof))
...
> Thanks for the report! Fixed in c27fe369, going out in the next release.
> 
> The problem was that `ultracite init` inlined the plugin-filtering logic (including a `typeof plugin === "string"` check) directly into the generated `oxlint.config.ts`, so enabling the anti-slop preset flagged `anti-slop/no-runtime-typeof` on a file ultracite itself wrote — with no autofix available.
> 
> The generated config now uses a new `selectJsPlugins` export from `ultracite/oxlint/js-plugins` instead, so the filtering logic lives inside the package where it isn't linted:
> 
> ```ts
> import { defineConfig } from "oxlint";
> import core from "ultracite/oxlint/core";
> import { selectJsPlugins } from "ultracite/oxlint/js-plugins";
> 
> export default defineConfig({
> extends: [core, selectJsPlugins(["github", "sonarjs"])],
> ignorePatterns: core.ignorePatterns,
> });
> ```
> 
> Once the release is out, you can either re-run `npx ultracite init` (it migrates the old config automatically, preserving your plugin selection) or just replace the inlined block with the one-liner above.
Source: Ultracite Init breaks anti slop rules · Issue #770 (https://github.com/haydenbleasel/ultracite/issues/770)

